<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frère Jacques</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">melody c. 1780 (manuscript of <a href="/years/1775">1775</a>–<a href="/years/1785">1785</a>); words printed <a href="/years/1825">1825</a>, words and music together <a href="/years/1869">1869</a> (Lebouc's book <a href="/years/1860">1860</a>)</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Frère Jacques&quot;, known in English as &quot;Brother John&quot;, is a nursery rhyme of French origin, traditionally sung as a round. It is about a friar who has overslept and is urged to wake and ring the bell for matins, the night or early-morning prayers. The common English version keeps the rhythm but changes the meaning, so that the bells wake Brother John. The French frère, &quot;brother&quot;, is the root of the English word friar.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The earliest known version of the melody is in a French manuscript, titled Recueil de Timbres de Vaudevilles and held by the Bibliothèque Nationale, which the library dates between 1775 and 1785. There the tune is labelled Frère Blaise. A publication of 1825 printed the words with a description of the melody in solfège but without musical notation. The words and music appear together in Charles Lebouc's popular collection of children's rounds, Récréations de l'enfance, issued by Rouart, Lerolle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. The musicologist Sylvie Bouissou has found some evidence that Jean-Philippe Rameau wrote the music, since a manuscript at the French National Library lists him as author of this piece among 86 canons.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The French song asks a friar, Frère Jacques, whether he is sleeping and tells him to sound the bell for matins, the midnight or very early morning prayers. The usual English version, Brother John, keeps the rhythm but reverses the sense, so that the bells wake the sleeper. English friar comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-french">Old French</a> frere, which comes in turn from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> frater.</p><p>Several origins have been proposed. A link with the 17th-century surgeon Frère Jacques Beaulieu was explored without evidence, and Martine David and A. Marie Delrieu suggest the song mocked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-order">Dominican friars</a>, known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> as the Jacobin order, for sloth. Resemblances exist with a piece by Girolamo Frescobaldi first published around 1615, with a French song of 1650 and with a Hungarian folk tune.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The tune is one of the simplest repeating canons, like Three Blind Mice, and it has spread easily. In 1926 officers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/whampoa-military-academy">Whampoa Military Academy</a> used it for the patriotic song Revolution of the Citizens, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> it is also widely thought of as a folk song called Two Tigers. Gustav Mahler set it in a minor key in the third movement of his Symphony No. 1, calling it by its German name, Bruder Martin. Leonard Bernstein used it to explain counterpoint in his Young People's Concerts. The Beatles' Paperback Writer has the title sung beneath its last verse, and the French group Les Frères Jacques took its name from the song's friar.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Frère Jacques” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
